# Authentication

Apply when `PROJECT.md` declares an auth system. The chosen library, protected
routes, customer types, and signup side-effects are project-specific — see
`PROJECT.md`.

## Principles

- **Use a vetted auth library; never roll your own.** If it lacks a feature you
  need, document the gap and prefer a plugin or upstream PR over a custom system.
- **One auth system per project.** Don't mix two session models.
- **Keep auth portable**: isolate it behind your own helpers so the rest of the
  codebase depends on `getSession()` / `useSession()`, not on the vendor SDK
  directly. This makes the provider swappable.

## Reading the session

- **Server side** (server components / handlers / actions): read the session
  through a single server helper. Cache it per request to dedupe. Never read auth
  cookies directly.
- **Client side**: read through the auth library's client, wrapped in one custom
  hook — never via raw `fetch`.
- **Never pass the full session object to client components**; pass only the
  fields the client needs (id, display name).
- **Never log the session object** — it may contain PII.

```ts
// server helper — cache per request
export const getSession = cache(async () =>
  auth.api.getSession({ headers: await headers() }),
);
```

## Route protection

Protect private routes in middleware (or the framework equivalent), redirecting
unauthenticated users to the login page with a return path. Keep the protected
path list in one place and keep it in sync with the matcher.

```ts
const PROTECTED = ["/account", "/checkout"]; // see PROJECT.md for the real list
```

Double-check that confirmation / receipt pages are protected — an unauthenticated
user must not be able to view another user's data by URL.

## Cross-runtime auth (functions / different runtimes)

A function running in a different runtime usually cannot call the auth library
directly. Instead:

1. The trusted server mints a **signed, short-lived token** and passes it in an
   `Authorization: Bearer` header.
2. The function **verifies the token** against a shared secret, extracts the user
   id, and uses a privileged DB client for the work.

**Never forward raw session cookies across runtimes.** Always use a signed token.

## Signup / multi-step flows

When signup has side-effects (create profile row, sync to CRM, add to mailing
list, send welcome email), separate **critical** from **best-effort** steps:

- **Critical steps must be atomic** from the user's view: if creating the local
  profile fails, roll back the auth user.
- **Best-effort steps** (third-party sync, marketing list, email) must never fail
  the signup. Capture failures in the error tracker and continue; the user can
  retry from their profile.
- **Never block signup/login on a third party being reachable.** Run those
  asynchronously.

## Anonymous → authenticated continuity

If anonymous users accumulate state (a cart, a draft), merge it into their
account **on both signup and login**. Use the privileged client for the
cross-identity update and log the merge for traceability.

## Validation

Validate auth inputs with a schema (signup, login, profile, password reset).
For conditional fields (e.g. a company id required only for business accounts),
use a discriminated union so invalid combinations can't typecheck or submit.

## Password policy

Applies whenever the project accepts passwords (signup, password change, reset,
admin-created accounts). It follows NIST SP 800-63B and OWASP ASVS: length and
blocklists, not composition rules. The project's brand terms live in
`PROJECT.md` (section 8, "Password policy terms").

### Length and characters

- **Minimum 12 characters, maximum 128**; at least 64 must always be accepted.
  Count Unicode code points after NFKC normalization, not bytes, and normalize
  the same way before hashing.
- **Accept every character**: upper and lower case, digits, symbols, spaces,
  accents, any Unicode. Never trim or alter what the user typed (beyond NFKC).
- **No composition rules** (no mandatory uppercase, digit, or symbol) and **no
  periodic forced rotation**; force a change only on evidence of compromise.
- **Never truncate silently.** Too long is a validation error with a message.
  Bcrypt-backed providers cap input at 72 bytes (Supabase Auth rejects longer
  passwords): record that cap in `PROJECT.md` as the effective maximum, and do
  not hand-roll a pre-hash to get around it.
- **Paste and password managers allowed**: no `onpaste` blocking, correct
  `autocomplete="new-password"` / `"current-password"`, a show-password toggle,
  no maximum below the policy in the HTML `maxlength`.

### What is rejected

| Category | Examples |
|---|---|
| Known breached passwords | anything in Pwned Passwords |
| Common passwords | `password123`, `admin123456` |
| Dictionary words with predictable edits (multilingual, French included) | `Bonjour12345`, `Welcome2026!` |
| Keyboard and alphabetic sequences | `azerty123456`, `qwerty123456`, `abcdefghijkl` |
| Repetitions | `aaaaaaaaaaaa`, `123123123123` |
| Obvious substitutions | `P@ssw0rd2026!` |
| Brand, site, domain, or app name | `BeBranded2026!`, `B3Br@nded123`, `bebranded1234`, `examplecom123` |
| User's own data | email local part, username, first or last name, plus a year |

**Reject predictable passwords, not substrings.** A long, strong passphrase
that contains a dictionary word, a sequence, or the brand name is accepted. So
brand terms and user data are fed to the strength estimator as penalized
inputs; never implement them as a plain "contains" ban.

### How it is checked

One server-side `checkPassword(password, { userInputs })` function, shared by
every flow that sets a password, runs in this order:

1. **Length** (after normalization).
2. **Strength estimate with `zxcvbn-ts`** (`@zxcvbn-ts/core` with the common,
   English and French language packages, plus any other user-facing locale).
   Pass the brand terms from `PROJECT.md` and the user's data as
   `userInputs`; it already handles case changes and l33t substitutions
   (`a→@`, `o→0`, `e→3`, `i→1`, `s→$`). Reject below score 3 and return its
   feedback. The original `dropbox/zxcvbn` is unmaintained; use a maintained
   port in other languages (`zxcvbn-rs`, `zxcvbn-python`) with the same inputs.
3. **Breach check with Pwned Passwords**, k-anonymity range API only: send the
   first 5 hex characters of the SHA-1, compare suffixes locally, send the
   `Add-Padding: true` header. Never send the password or its full hash to any
   third party. Short timeout (2 s); on outage, fail open for this check only,
   fall back to a bundled list of the top common passwords (e.g. from
   SecLists), and record a metric. This follows "never block signup on a third
   party" above; the length and strength checks always run.

- **Server-side is authoritative.** The client may run the same estimator for
  live feedback (lazy-load it; it is large), but the server re-checks.
- **Brand terms are built, not hard-coded in logic**: commercial name, domain
  and its variants (`example.com`, `examplecom`, `example`), app and product
  names, acronyms, relevant internal service names. They are data from
  `PROJECT.md`, loaded into the dictionary at startup.
- **Messages are specific and kind**: say why (too short, found in a breach,
  too close to the site name) and suggest a passphrase. Never echo the password.
- **Hosted auth providers**: enable the provider's own length and breach checks
  too (Supabase: `minimum_password_length = 12`, empty
  `password_requirements`, leaked password protection on). Provider endpoints
  called directly from a client bypass the custom check: route every
  password-setting flow through the server (admin API or server action) when
  the full policy must be guaranteed, and document any gap in `PROJECT.md`.

### Storage

- **Argon2id** when the project hashes passwords itself: OWASP minimum
  (m=19 MiB, t=2, p=1) or stronger, tuned to ~250-500 ms on production
  hardware, a per-password random salt, parameters stored in the encoded hash.
  Use the library's encoded format and constant-time verify.
- **Rehash on login** when stored parameters are below the current ones.
- When the auth library or provider owns hashing (bcrypt, scrypt), keep its
  vetted algorithm; do not wrap it in a custom scheme.
- Passwords never reach logs, error trackers, analytics, or third parties in
  clear form (see Security below).

## Security

- HttpOnly cookies for tokens; never `localStorage`. HTTPS-only in production.
- Enforce the password policy above in the shared server check and in the auth config.
- Rate-limit signup and login (e.g. a few attempts per IP per window).
- Require email verification before sensitive actions if the project needs it.
- Never log passwords, tokens, or sessions; scrub before sending anywhere.
- Cascade deletes so deleting an auth user doesn't orphan the profile row.

## Email sending

If the project has its own email provider, override the auth library's default
sender so all emails (verification, password reset) go through it for consistent
branding, tracking, and unsubscribe handling.

## Testing

Auth is a maximal-testing area (`testing.mdc`, row "Auth / session / permissions"):
- **Authentication suite**: signup, login, logout, wrong password, locked
  account, expired session, refresh, password reset token single-use and
  expiry, email verification, rate limiting (N+1 attempts are rejected).
- **Authorization suite**: every protected route and action tested as
  unauthenticated, as the owner, and as another authenticated user; IDOR on
  every id-bearing URL; role escalation attempts.
- **Signup side-effects**: critical step failure rolls back the auth user;
  best-effort step failure still completes signup; anonymous state merges on
  signup and on login.
- **Cross-runtime tokens**: expired, wrong signature, wrong audience, replayed.
- **Cookies**: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite asserted in an integration test.
- **Password policy table tests**: 11 chars rejected, 12 accepted; 128
  accepted, 129 rejected with a message (never truncated); emoji, accents and
  spaces accepted; one rejected example per category of the table above,
  including brand variants (`BeBranded2026!`, `B3Br@nded123`, the domain without
  its dot) and user data; a long random passphrase containing the brand name or
  a dictionary word accepted; every flow (signup, change, reset, admin create)
  goes through the same check.
- **Breach check**: the request carries only a 5-character SHA-1 prefix and the
  padding header (asserted on the mocked HTTP call); a matched suffix rejects;
  timeout and 5xx fall back to the bundled list without blocking signup.
- **Hashing**: stored value is an Argon2id encoded hash with the configured
  parameters (or the provider's format); a weaker stored hash is upgraded on
  next login; no password appears in captured logs or error events.
- **Schema fuzzing** of every auth input; only validation errors result.
- **E2E**: the full login, signup, reset, and logout journeys on desktop and
  mobile viewports; no PII in logs asserted with a log capture.

## Checklist when touching auth code
- [ ] Session read through the shared helper; cached server-side
- [ ] No PII logged (password, token, email)
- [ ] Middleware protects every required route (including confirmation pages)
- [ ] Critical signup steps roll back on failure; side-effects are non-blocking
- [ ] Anonymous state merged on signup AND login
- [ ] Inputs validated with a schema
- [ ] Rate limiting in place
- [ ] Every password-setting flow calls the shared server check (length 12-128,
      zxcvbn-ts score >= 3 with brand terms and user data, Pwned Passwords range API)
- [ ] No composition rules, no silent truncation, paste and password managers allowed
- [ ] Brand terms in `PROJECT.md` are current; provider-side length and breach checks on
- [ ] Argon2id (or the provider's vetted hash), rehash on login when parameters rise
- [ ] `.env.example` updated for new variables
- [ ] Tests cover happy path AND failure paths
